herdr agent wait adv-codex --timeout 1500000
herdr agent wait wgka --timeout 3600000
```

**4. Backlog.** Edit `orchestrator/backlog.md` ("B8 open 01:40: relay read timeout; adv-fable asked, adv-codex queued behind B7; wgka continues, provisional 5 s"), run `orchestrator/build-backlog.sh` in the foreground, then update the Artifact.

**5. Chat note to the operator** (no question)
> 01:40 status, nothing to answer. The executor building the WireGuard keepalive relay (the small nginx listener that answers the VPN client's "still alive" request, so tunnels stop rebuilding every ~45 s) asked which read timeout to use. That went to the two advisors, and work continues meanwhile. Tonight I will reach you only for the steps that are yours: the merge go, and the canary acceptance with your own WireGuard profile.

## When woken

**6. adv-codex settles.** Foreground: `herdr agent read adv-codex --source recent-unwrapped --lines 200`, and file the B7 result. Then send the same prompt as step 2 to `adv-codex`, followed by a background `herdr agent wait adv-codex --timeout 1200000`.

**7. Both votes in, 2/2.** Foreground:
```
herdr agent prompt wgka "B8 closed 2/2: Option <N>. Make it final, remove the PROVISIONAL mark, finish, then report for cross-review. Reply in English."
```
On a split I break no tie; it becomes the operator's poll.

## Acceptance

**8.** No poll now. After a clean Codex review, the merge poll ends with: "After your go the deploy to ccs-it takes about 15 minutes; then I need 3 more minutes of you for the acceptance ping."

After the deploy from `main` and green gateway checks via `-J root@88.99.219.20`, my last action is an AskUserQuestion:

> The keepalive relay is live on ccs-it and the server-side checks are green. The last check is yours: connect with your own WireGuard profile for ccs-it and run `ping -i 0.2 172.27.96.2` for 3 minutes. What did you see?
> 1. Passed: no loss, no freezes. Accept the canary.
> 2. Failed: loss or a freeze.
> 3. Later: I'll run it in the morning. Keep it on ccs-it only.

## Why

- **The timeout is mine to route, not to decide.** The standing rule gives it to the two advisors. My agreement with Option 1 and last night's 2/2 predict the result but carry no authority. It is also the kind of trivia the operator asked not to be woken for.
- **The ballot costs no schedule.** `adv-fable` starts now and `adv-codex` gets it the moment B7 ends; interrupting would throw away a review. The executor keeps working on a labelled, one-line-reversible value that cannot leave the worktree before the vote.
- **The ballot text is neutral.** It carries no recommendation and no precedent, so the two votes are independent.
- **Every wait is in the background.** A foreground wait would freeze the session for up to 10 minutes while three tabs need me.
- **Acceptance is the operator's alone.** I don't hold their profile, and a probe of `172.27.96.2` from the workstation is forbidden as evidence (hard rule #17). It is not trivia, so it gets one prepared sitting, chained to the merge go, and asked last because a poll blocks my session. Option 3 keeps an unaccepted canary confined to `ccs-it`.
